<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gwanggaeto the Great</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">374–413</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Gwanggaeto of Goguryeo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gwanggaeto the Great was the nineteenth king of Goguryeo, reigning from 391 to 413. He conquered wide lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> from the Later Yan and the Khitan, defeated Baekje, and sent an army to help Silla against Wa (Japanese) and Gaya forces in 400. His deeds are recorded on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gwanggaeto-stele">Gwanggaeto Stele</a>, erected by his son Jangsu in 414 near the old capital in modern Ji'an, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gwanggaeto-the-great.jpg" alt="Gwanggaeto the Great" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: EditQ · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gwanggaeto_Stele_5.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gwanggaeto was born in 374, three years after the kingdom of Baekje under Geunchogo defeated Goguryeo, killed King Gogukwon and sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyongyang">Pyongyang</a>. His father was King Gogukyang and his grandfather Gogukwon. Goguryeo avoided conflict with Baekje in these years and cultivated good relations with the Former Qin, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> and the Rouran. Gogukwon's successor Sosurim made peace with Baekje, spread <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> through the kingdom's institutions and carried out military reforms after the defeats by Baekje and the Xianbei. Gogukyang then invaded Later Yan in 385 and Baekje in 386. These arrangements prepared the ground for Gwanggaeto's expansion.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Gwanggaeto took the throne in 391, adopting the era name Yeongnak and the title Taewang, equivalent to emperor, which presented him as the equal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s rulers. In 392 he attacked Baekje with 40,000 troops and took ten walled cities. Baekje's king Asin counterattacked in 393 and invaded again in 394 but was defeated both times. In 396 Gwanggaeto assaulted the Baekje capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wiryeseong">Wiryeseong</a> by land and by sea along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-river">Han River</a>, and Asin submitted, surrendering a prince and ten ministers.</p><p>In the west and north he destroyed three tribes and between 600 and 700 camps of the Khitan Baili clan on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-river">Liao River</a> in 395, and conquered the Sushen in 398. In 400 Silla asked for help against an invasion by Baekje, Gaya and Wa. Gwanggaeto sent 50,000 troops and defeated the coalition, after which Silla stood under his influence and Gaya declined. In 402 he returned Prince Silseong, held as a hostage since 392, to Silla as its king.</p><p>He beat back Later Yan attacks, took a fortress near its capital in 402 and conquered all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a>, which Goguryeo held until the mid to late 7th century. In 404 he defeated a Wa attack on the former Daifang commandery, and in 410 he attacked Eastern Buyeo.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Gwanggaeto died of an unknown illness and was succeeded by his eldest son Jangsu. He is credited with taking 64 walled cities and 1,400 villages. His deeds are recorded on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gwanggaeto-stele">Gwanggaeto Stele</a>, a monument 6.39 m tall that Jangsu erected in 414 at the supposed site of his tomb in Ji'an, and which was rediscovered in the late 19th century. Not all its characters survive, and Korean and Japanese scholars disagree on how to read its passages about the Wa. Koreans regard him as a hero and he is often taken as a symbol of Korean nationalism.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gwanggaeto the Great” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its early wars against Goguryeo culminated in Geunchogo's victory at Pyongyang in 371, but under <b>Gwanggaeto the Great</b> Goguryeo struck back, taking dozens of Baekje fortresses in 396 and forcing King Asin to submit.</blockquote><a href="/empire/baekje">Baekje · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Gwanggaeto the Great</b> (r. 391–413) campaigned against Baekje, the Later Yan, the Khitan and eastern Buyeo, and in 400 sent an army to relieve Silla from Wa and Gaya attackers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/goguryeo">Goguryeo · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gungnae">Gungnae</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/north-korea">North Korea</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.12,126.18&amp;year=399" aria-label="Gungnae on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Gwanggaeto the Great"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5397 4044h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5397 4044h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.12,126.18&amp;year=399">What was here in 399 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
